WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
[ Music ]

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.420
[Narrator:] It's rare indeed that these two
world-famous men can be brought together in a film.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.240
Dr. Joslin died in 1962 at the age of 93.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:20.599
But fortunately, Dr. Best, who with Dr. Banting discovered insulin, is still with us.

00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:23.880
When one realizes that there are some one and one-half million

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:30.149
diabetics in the United States, and another like amount yet undiscovered, or when at any time

00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:33.040
you or a member of your family can be afflicted
with

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:39.580
this disease, then and then only can one comprehend
the full value of the contributions of

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.660
Dr. Joslin, who devoted a lifetime to perfecting the

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:49.719
treatment of diabetes, and of Dr. Best the
co-discoverer of life-giving insulin.

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:55.940
[Dr. Root:] Whether you boys know Dr. Best...Dr. Charles Best, who's coming here today,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.670
is the famous doctor who was the co-discoverer

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.510
with Sir Frederick Banting of insulin 40 years ago.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:09.820
He's coming here to take part and to speak
at the Elliott P. Joslin Memorial in Oxford.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.610
He was a friend of Dr. Joslin's for many years.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.700
[Howey:] Dr. Joslin was my doctor.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.540
He was great.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:18.660
I don't know...

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:24.780
You knew he was an old man and yet, somehow, he wasn't like a lot of other old people.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.980
[Dr. Root:] Well, he was 93 when he died.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:34.220
[Scott:] Yes, but he was always asking questions about our diabetes.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.820
Why, he even visited us here at camp just
the summer before.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.400
[ Car horn ]

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:46.320
[ Music ]

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.180
[Dr. Best:] Dr. Garmin, I know that you and
I agree on the tremendous importance of teaching

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.580
these boys good control of their diabetes.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.500
As a matter of fact, you should set them a
pretty good example, I think,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.000
you had diabetes yourself.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.180
[Dr. Garmin] Well, there are three things,
Dr. Best.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.420
First, the very close care that my mother
gave me when I was a child.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:18.520
And second, the almost imperceptible transfer of responsibility during my adolescence.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:24.760
And finally, the factor common at all times
was the close association and friendship

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.080
with my physician.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.960
[Dr. Root:] Dr. Best, I'd like to have
you meet one of Dr. Joslin's former patients,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:31.970
Scott Currier.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.520
[Dr. Best:] Scott, how did you happen to come to this camp?

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.360
[Scott:] Dr. White.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.920
She's at the clinic too. She suggested it to
my folks.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.930
Dr. Joslin always  used to tell me how important it was to take care of my own diabetes.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.380
[Howey:] He told everyone that.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.960
That's one reason he started the camp, to
teach diabetics how to take care of themselves.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.540
[Dr. Joslin:] We have had a wonderful group
of patients.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:08.300
To all of those who had lived longer with 
their diabetes than they were expected to live if they didn't have it,

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:13.960
Ms. Amelia Peabody made a life expectancy medal.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:19.890
This was given now to two or three thousand patients,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:29.340
and they have cherished it because it showed how faithfully they had lived.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:36.410
But so many of them obtained it that later
we had another medal for patients.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:43.090
And this was given to those who after 25 years of the disease

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:55.130
had perfect blood vessels, perfect eyesight,
and perfect kidneys, and were generally sound in health.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:59.150
Of these, we have had now some 98 patients.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:05.760
Not one who has obtained this medal of perfection,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:18.880
ever was able to obtain it unless he had followed treatment meticulously just as [?] and Alan prescribed.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.020
[Dr. Best:] Scott, what do you mean when you say take care of yourself?

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.320
[Howey:] Well, I think I can show you better.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.660
Come on over to the clinic and we'll show you.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.500
Hey, tell Stevie to come here a minute.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.479
Steve is the youngest boy in camp who takes
his own insulin injection, Dr. Best.

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:42.819
He's only seven and already he's learned to
take care of himself this way.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:43.760
[Camp staffer:] Okay, boys.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.760
[Steve:] What you want? I got to take my shot!

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.700
[Howey:] Hey, Steve, how about giving Dr.
Best a performance?

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.020
[Steve:] Well, that's that.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:13.380
Me and Art have to go and eat.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.720
[Howey:] He's a cute kid but boy, he can
be a pill.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.940
Now we'll never hear the end of it: "I shot
myself for Dr. Best."

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.500
[Scott:] Years ago when doctors didn't know
what they know now,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.039
the kids just didn't get very tall.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:32.509
[Howey:] Sure, I know that.

00:05:32.509 --> 00:05:34.669
They used to call them diabetic dwarfs.

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:38.460
[Scott:] It was because they didn't get enough protein in their diet.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.490
Now we know we must take care of our height,
our weight, as well as our diet.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.419
[Dr. Best:] You mean that you weigh and measure your food and know how to prepare it?

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:51.840
[Scott:] Here, let me show you in the diet
kitchen.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:58.440
I have a diet of so much fat, protein, and
carbohydrate and I use one of these scales

00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.360
to measure it.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.770
I check myself about once a week now but I've been doing it for so long,

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:09.680
I think my estimates are perfectly all right,
at least the doctor and the dietitian think so.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:17.719
[Dr. Best:] Do you find, Scott, that it takes
a lot of time to weigh your food and take 
 your insulin shots?

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.140
Do you find it hard to do?

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.650
[Scott:] Well, at first it was sort of a hard
thing and then I became used to it.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.920
And now it's nothing at all.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:30.880
[Howey:] It's really not even as hard as seventh-grade
math.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:32.379
[Scott:] Hi, Betty.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:36.100
I was just showing Dr. Best how we weigh and measure our food,

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.320
the kind of thing that Dr. Joslin used to teach us.

00:06:39.339 --> 00:06:43.599
[Betty:] He was always interested in the children knowing how to do these things.

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:45.289
Howey, where's Beetle?

00:06:45.289 --> 00:06:49.860
[Howey:] I think he's down by the lake. [Betty:] Well, when you see him, would you give this to him?

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.220
[Howey:] Wow! Sure!

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.940
This is for Beetle Daws. He's bugs about bugs.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.920
[Scott:] You know, this camp is for fun too.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.240
We do everything they do at any camp.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.420
You don't have to feel you're sitting on a
bench if you're diabetic.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:49.700
[Saxophone music, kids clapping ]

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.440
[Dr. Best:] You know boys with all this activity around the camp, makes me think of

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.480
another one of Dr. Joslin's great interests.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:08:01.660
He was always trying to find out the influence of exercise on diabetes.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:05.660
[Dr. Joslin:] Now, Bouchardat found this,

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:09.949
and this puzzled him and embarrassed him.

00:08:09.949 --> 00:08:19.820
He found that the patients to whom he gave a diet--the same diet that he had in the hospital--and sent them home,

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:29.820
that they did better when they were away from him at home on their farms than when he had them in a hospital.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:32.460
And it finally dawned upon him

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.860
that exercise was the reason for it.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:37.960
[Dr. Best:] Thank you, Scott.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.360
Thank you, too, for acting as the host at
the camp for us today.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:49.380
[Howey:] There's a girls' camp, the Clara
Barton camp, that has a program just like ours.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.440
Boy, won't they flip when they find out that
you've been here and not there.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:59.600
[Dr. Best:] Well, as matter of fact, I'm going
over to the Clara Barton camp tomorrow after the dedication.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:02.900
Are you boys going to be at the dedication?

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:05.360
[Scott:] You bet!I wouldn't miss that for anything.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.540
[Howey:] Me too.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:16.580
[ Music ]

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:23.860
[Dr. Root:] We are meant today to celebrate
the 250th anniversary of the founding of Oxford, Massachusetts.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:32.900
In this city were born two persons who achieved distinction by their service to humanity.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:40.280
Clara Barton. Born in Oxford in 1821.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:44.790
She brought about the formation of the United States branch of the International Red Cross.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:50.720
Dr. Elliott P. Joslin, born here in 1869,
became a professor of medicine,

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.300
a distinguished physician, known the world over for his teachings in diabetes.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:00.560
But my real privilege today is the introduction of Dr. Charles H. Best.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:02.760
[Dr. Best:] Thank you Dr. Root.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:07.680
I enjoyed every time I ever met Dr. Elliott
P. Joslin.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.760
And I'm delighted to have this opportunity
to pay a tribute to his memory.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:37.580
[ Music ]

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:47.120
[Susie:] Dr. Best, many of us knew Dr. Joslin
rather well too.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.300
And your speech at Oxford really meant a lot to us.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.079
Some of the things you said

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:53.890
were just the way we felt about him.

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:55.560
[Dr. Best:] Thank you, Susie.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:59.540
Dr. Joslin knew how to say things wonderfully well.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:04.920
I've enjoyed having the tour of the Clara Barton camp with you three girls.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:08.740
And I'm amazed at how many things you've learned to do well.

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:14.120
[Cathy:] We've been wondering just what did happen to the children in the days before insulin?

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:15.640
What was it like?

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:21.280
[Dr. Best:] Well, Cathy, there were thousands of years after diabetes was discovered

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:24.140
when there wasn't much you could do for children.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:28.800
You fed them as little as possible and they
lived for a time.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.680
Then in 1921 and '22, when we had insulin,

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:36.140
the first children did very well.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:38.480
But we didn't have much insulin.

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:44.700
And with one or two of the children, we ran
out of insulin and we lost a little girl.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.520
One little girl and one boy.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:52.360
[Susie:] You know, Dr. Best, that Dr. Joslin
took care of my mother?

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.180
[Dr. Best:] Yeah.

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:57.540
[Susie:] In fact he warned her that I had a good chance of becoming a diabetic.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:02.920
[Dr. Best:] Yes, we know Susie that diabetes does run in families.

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:06.700
and it's passed down from the mother or the father to some of the children.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:13.380
Linda, someone told me that you've been in
the camp here for seven summers.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:15.000
You'd come for seven summers.

00:12:15.019 --> 00:12:17.930
[Linda:] Yes, I've been coming to Clara Barton for seven summers now.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:20.699
[Dr. Best:] And what are your parents think
about your coming?

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.860
Do you think that they're very pleased?

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.640
Have you learned something that gives them satisfaction?

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:29.380
[Linda:] Yes. They're pleased that I've come to camp.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:34.939
Because by coming to camp, I've learned to
take care of myself by adjusting my diet and my insulin.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:39.040
Taking the right amount of exercise so I can control my diabetes by myself.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:39.820
[ Dr. Best:] Yeah.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:40.660
[Linda:] Which puts them at ease.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:44.400
Because when I go away from home, they won't have to worry about me.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:45.520
[Dr. Best:] Well, I think you're right, Linda.

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:48.660
This is a wonderful camp.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:50.800
I've been to a lot of other camps.

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:58.060
And after thinking about them, I feel that
they do a great deal not only for the children

00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:58.980
but for the parents.

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:05.980
Well, girls, Dr. Joslin was very much interested in these diabetic camps, too.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.900
He used to talk about them when I saw him
in Boston.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:16.560
And I remember he mentioned them when came to Toronto to see the opening of our research institute.

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:21.560
Dr. Joslin was interested in investigation,
you know, research.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.380
And he was interested in the camps.

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:26.900
And he told us at that time

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:31.959
that he hoped someday he'd have a wonderful clinic where he could do all or many things

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:34.560
that he had in his mind to do for diabetics.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:42.040
Well, as you know, that clinic really has
come into being and I hope to go there again tomorrow.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:52.200
[Dr. Joslin:] To predict the future diabetic,
we hope to erect in Boston a hospital-teaching clinic

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:58.140
in which ambulatory diabetics can return at much less

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:03.280
than regular hospital costs, fully a
third less...

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:14.300
receive instruction, encouragement, and such development of character that life and health

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:20.020
hitherto unrecorded for diabetics will be
obtained.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:36.540
[ Music ]

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:42.660
[Dr. Best:] It's nearly 10 years ago now since Dr. Joslin came to Toronto to take part

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:46.340
in the opening of the institute which they named after me.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:52.780
Two years later, the cornerstone of this Joslin clinic was laid.

00:14:53.740 --> 00:15:01.140
and here we are now in the completed clinic which Dr. Joslin had so much desired.

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:05.360
[Dr. Root:] Yes, Dr. Best.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:11.660
This clinic has fulfilled not only the ideals
which he expressed there in founding

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:13.750
your institute, but also the hopes which

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:17.980
you know he had as long ago as 1922 when you first came to Boston.

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:26.209
And here are the opportunities for training
patients. An adequate and proper diet is

00:15:26.209 --> 00:15:29.960
more important today in the treatment of diabetes than ever before.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:39.080
Here we emphasize doctors teaching patients how to measure and evaluate food.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:47.980
When a child develops diabetes, it helps tremendously if the child and her mother can together

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:57.100
in the same room be treated, and the mother learn firsthand the best methods for the care of that child.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:03.000
Perhaps the most important single thing for a diabetic patient to learn is how to

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.240
test the urine for sugar and when to do it.

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:10.399
One important aspect of our program is the

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:12.320
doctors' teaching of patients.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:15.100
Mrs. Cohan.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:21.440
You have known the teaching and training in diabetes over many years which Dr. Joslin inculcated.

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.700
May I ask you how you feel about it?

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.040
[Mrs.Cohan:] When I first came here 25 years ago

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:35.990
with my daughter, who was then a child diabetic and myself; I was worried, alarmed,

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.200
tortured how I was going to care for both
herself and myself.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:46.680
After being a week at the clinic in the teaching unit, I learned how to take care of us.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:54.460
I learned all about diet and food, exercise,
the care of the infant to such a great degree

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:57.440
that I was completely in confidence.

00:16:57.449 --> 00:17:02.350
I had the absolute confidence that I can go home and take care of all my problems.

00:17:02.350 --> 00:17:06.600
My fears were gone, and I felt that I could
do all that was necessary

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:08.480
for the best of both of us.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:13.900
[Woman speaker 1:] Can you have diabetes without having any apparent symptoms?

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:17.720
[Dr. Krall:] Why, yes, of course.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:21.530
Very early in diabetes sometimes there are
no symptoms and you have to find elevated

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:22.940
blood sugars or urine sugars.

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:26.800
[Male speaker 1:] Is necessary to keep  insulin in the refrigerator?

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:28.400
[Dr. Krall:] The insulin
you are storing should be

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.040
kept in the refrigerator.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:32.830
But the insulin that you're using can be kept
at room temperature.

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:37.740
[Woman speaker 2:] If one member of a family has diabetes, should the other members

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.500
of the same family be checked?

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.000
[Dr. Krall:] Why, certainly.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:47.460
Diabetes has hereditary tendencies and other members of family should be checked

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:50.240
particularly those whose are overweight, if any.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:54.860
[Male speaker 2:] These pills sound exciting. Can all diabetics use these pills?

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:56.300
[Dr. Krall:] No, I'm afraid not.

00:17:56.300 --> 00:18:00.960
While many of the  diabetics who are milder get by with pills perfectly well,

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:05.100
most diabetics and certainly more severe ones still require insulin.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:10.880
[Dr. Best:] We all agree that one of the most
important things in looking after diabetics

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:11.880
is to teach them.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.820
I know that this has done extremely well in the clinic here.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:21.300
I've seen Dr. Joslin, you, Howard, and many others

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:24.290
giving a great deal of their time to patient
education.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:31.420
Dr. Root, I know that Dr. Joslin has been
interested for many, many years in training

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:37.060
physicians in the various aspects of diabetes, and that they've come to him from all over the world

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:38.320
[Dr. Root:] Yes.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:42.200
And today we have a group of doctors from
many countries

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:44.400
in various types of training.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.540
In fact, they have a number of questions to
ask you, Dr. Best.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:54.040
[Doctor 1:] I've always wondered, Dr. Best,
how you happen to find insulin and many other

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:55.940
researchers have failed?

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:02.980
[Dr. Best:] I suppose there's an element of
good fortune in all successful research.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:06.920
We had several assets really.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.570
We had good surgery.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:12.980
Fred Banting was an excellent surgeon.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:21.020
And then we had the micro methods for doing blood sugar which all our predecessors had lacked.

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:23.500
And then we had youth.

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:27.980
We weren't too much concerned about what happened before we started.

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:32.980
[Doctor 2:] Dr. Best, you have a long-range
picture of the diabetes mellitus problem.

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:35.240
Could you tell us what advances you hope will be made

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:40.840
for the diabetic patient in the next 10 years? [Dr.Best:] Well, of course, no one can be

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:48.260
completely certain about any research problem--about the solution of any research problem until

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.140
it's actually accomplish.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:52.580
But I think the indications

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:58.110
are that we will have synthetic insulin.It
won't be any different perhaps in potency

00:19:58.110 --> 00:19:59.800
than the natural insulin.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:05.180
But we're going to need some day more insulin than you can make from natural sources.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:11.040
[Doctor 3] I think we all like to be interested,
Dr. Best, to know how you went about getting

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:14.480
the first insulin and also getting enough
of it?

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:18.560
[Dr. Best:] Well, our first insulin was made
from dog pancreas.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:23.640
It was very good material, but of course you can't collect a lot of pancreas from dogs.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:26.610
Then we went to beef pancreas.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:29.270
And this proved to be an excellent source.

00:20:29.270 --> 00:20:34.420
We had to change the technique of extraction a bit, use different strengths of alcohol

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:35.960
and acid and so on.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:40.840
But the main problem at that time was to get into large-scale production.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:47.480
We did some of the preliminary work in the
University of Toronto, but we were fortunate

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:54.160
to have the cooperation of one of the great family companies in the United States, the Eli Lilly company.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:59.980
And their engineers were expert in doing the sort of thing that needed to be done.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:07.680
I remember that I went there nine times in
the first year to try to tell them what I knew,

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:10.780
and they taught us a very great deal.

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:18.120
And I think the insulin committee of the University of Toronto was very happy that we selected

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:21.740
the Eli Lily company to work with us for the
first,

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:28.080
preliminary, difficult year in the large-scale
production of insulin.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:37.180
[Doctor 4:] Dr. Best, in your travel around
the world, do you find the society of free people

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:40.960
developing programs in the field of diabetes?

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:46.870
[Dr. Best:] Well, Dr. Joslin and I had the
honor of being the first honorary presidents

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:49.060
of the International Diabetes Federation.

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:57.860
And over the years, I've been in a great many countries where societies devoted to the interest  of diabetics have been formed.

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:02.680
In some countries, these societies are doing a wonderful work.

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:06.160
And in others, of course, it's slow.

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:11.800
But the idea of the International Diabetes
Federation is to use the knowledge gained

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:22.710
in the more active countries and to apply
this in the  effort to help those countries which are not so fortunate.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:29.420
Dr. Lozano, you're finishing your two years
here in the clinic and you're going back to Mexico.

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:33.460
Do you feel that you will be able to apply
a lot of

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:36.830
the new information which you gained while
you were here?

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:39.320
[Dr. Lozano:] Yes, I do.

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:45.420
I will work in the Hospital of Nutrition Disease where, like many other places in Mexico,

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:47.760
we have a high standard of medicine.

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:54.760
Of course, there are areas in Latin America,
as in other parts of the world,

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:59.180
where getting enough food for the children is a special problem.

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:04.300
[Dr. Root:] And finally, now, his best hope,
his deepest hope--

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.400
a new laboratory for research.

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:12.940
One of Dr. Joslin's most absorbing interests in the last 20 years has been

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:17.640
the study and prevention of blood vessel disease.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:20.980
Dr. Reese, you are conducting research in
this field. Tell us about it.

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:24.720
[Dr. Reese:] Our research has been directed at the relationship

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:30.100
between the control of diabetes and the progression of small blood vessel disease

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:36.260
Studies today clearly indicate that good control retards the progression

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:39.000
of small blood vessel disease in diabetes.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:44.980
Another aspect of our research focuses upon the blood vessels of children who have

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:48.600
a high index of chance of developing diabetes.

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:54.590
With this camera, we can pick up abnormalities in a blood vessel before there's any evidence

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:57.500
of diabetes by the usual clinical criteria.

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:07.260
[Dr. Best:] I suppose, Dr. Root, that this
clinic has a very special meaning for you personally.

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:11.100
You are Dr. Joslin's first associate,

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:16.880
and I believe that you gave the first dose
of insulin that was administered in New England.

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:18.580
[Dr. Root:] Yes, Charlie.

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:22.180
But let me say this.. Even before the discovery of insulin,

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:27.290
Dr. Joslin had demonstrated that devotion
to the care of the patient,

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:31.140
that keen interest in learning and in teaching.

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:42.600
When we learned that you and Dr. Banting had each given yourselves a dose of insulin,

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:44.300
although neither of you had diabetes...

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:45.520
[Dr. Best:] Right.

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:56.360
[Dr. Root:] I should not have been so terrified as I was on that day, on August 7, 1922, when there stood before me this frail nurse...

00:24:56.370 --> 00:25:01.360
the diabetes which had reduced her weight
from 135 pounds to 65 pounds.

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:03.500
How much insulin should I give her?

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.420
[Dr. Best:] How much did you actually give
her?

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:10.080
[Dr. Root:] I have to admit, Charlie, I gave her one unit and my heart beat.

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:11.720
[Dr. Best:] Yeah.

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:14.780
[Dr. Root:] And there, there she is.

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:18.910
The nurse who learned to give insulin.

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:20.630
She was then the lovely Miss Peterson,

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:21.900
and now Mrs. Simons.

00:25:22.540 --> 00:25:25.620
[Dr. Best:] Mrs. Simons, what do you remember of that period in 1922?

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:31.860
[Mrs. Simons:] I remember particularly the
children because they started to

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:34.960
grow and thrive and live happily.

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:42.840
[Dr. Root:] And in those next months, she
was the nurse first instructed

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:46.020
to teach the administration of insulin among the nurses

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.020
of the Deacons Hospital.

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:54.380
[Dr. Best:] Dr. Elliott Proctor Joslin was
one of the greatest men whom I've ever known.

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:57.820
I heard his name when I was seven years old.

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:05.320
I met him in Toronto in 1922 and I was in
very close touch with him throughout the rest of his life.

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:08.960
I'm sure that I speak for everyone who's

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:11.120
taken a part in making this record.

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:13.840
We all feel highly honored.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:21.670
[Narrator:] And so, since 1922, with the discovery of insulin by doctors Banting and Best,

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:24.220
and the leadership of Dr. Elliott P Joslin

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:29.800
in development of a program of patient care, there has evolved a new life,

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:35.220
a normal life for millions of diabetics and their families.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:43.100
[ All singing ]

